  <HOMEWORK title="Homework 5: Fails only when the neighbour is active" prompt="Two channels share this board. Channel A tests clean by itself on the bench, but fails BER testing once channel B (right next to it) is active. The routing looks fine at a glance - find out what's coupling in."
            hint="Check the Trace Channel's Readouts tab for the crosstalk/aggressor numbers (SXR), and look at how closely the routing runs next to the aggressor lane on the board view."
            explanation="The trace was routed hugging the noisy aggressor lane for a long parallel run (no lane-aware routing was used), so the aggressor's pickup dominates the differential noise budget - the SXR (signal-to-crosstalk ratio) readout is poor even though the trace's own loss and skew are fine. This is a textbook &quot;crosstalk from an adjacent trace, via, or connector pin&quot; bring-up bug: it only shows up when the aggressor is actually toggling, exactly like a neighbouring lane's traffic-dependent failure in the field. Re-routing with more spacing (or adding shielding/guard traces) away from the aggressor lane fixes it."/>
</LABCHAIN>
